 A driver writes into the game's memory, so check the code it relies on before it writes anything — Yakumo compares every instruction and constant it depends on at start-up, from `patch_loaded_image`, and stays out, saying why, if one differs.
 
+## Host audio tracks
+
+A port can play an audio file of the host's own (MP3, Ogg Vorbis, Opus, FLAC or WAV) where the game plays one of its ATRAC tracks, with no ATRAC encoding: music a player adds, for example. `host/audio/host_audio.hpp` has the pieces. `register_host_track(id, track)` names the file and its loop (the whole file, a range in samples of the file or in seconds, its own `LOOPSTART`/`LOOPLENGTH` or `LOOPEND` tags, or none) under a 32-bit id the port chooses. `virtual_atrac_file(id, block_align)` is a stand-in ATRAC3plus WAVE file for it: the track's length and loop in its `fact` and `smpl` chunks, a private `PKHA` chunk naming the id, and frames of silence. Serve it where the game opens its music, usually with a file provider (`hle_extension::add_file_provider`). sceAtrac recognises the chunk and takes each frame from the decoded file instead of the ATRAC decoder; the game's buffer, its reads, `sceAtracAddStreamData`, loop counts and reset positions run as for its own tracks. A file is decoded to 44,100 Hz 16-bit stereo the first time it is needed (`prefetch_host_track` does it on a worker thread) and kept while it plays; the last three decoded are kept. It needs FFmpeg's libavformat and libswresample, which the bundled FFmpeg has; `<prefix>_NO_HOST_AUDIO` turns the whole facility off.
+
 ## Generated code stays out of the repository
 
 Recompiled code is a translation of the game's own executable, so it is derived from copyrighted material. Every player generates it from their own copy. Make that reproducible by documenting the executable identity and hash the profile expects, the exact generator command, and any deterministic pass that follows it.
